# Claude Code Insights

34 sessions total · 19 analyzed · 149 messages · 14h · 4 commits
2026-02-12 to 2026-02-13

## At a Glance

**What's working:** You're treating Claude Code as a full extensible platform — building out custom skills, installing dozens of slash commands, and wiring up session persistence tooling like /handover. Your real production work is impressive too: recovering stalled VFX pipelines, shipping a complete Squarespace-to-Vercel migration, and running CUTLASS kernel optimization all show you're using Claude for high-stakes work, not just experimentation. See _Impressive Things You Did_.

**What's hindering you:** On Claude's side, it repeatedly starts work in the wrong place — editing deployed copies instead of source repos, spawning redundant processes before checking what's already running — which costs you rework cycles. On your side, your Windows environment (cp1252 encoding, Git Bash escaping quirks, UAC elevation timing) silently derails execution regularly, and without a persistent reference doc for these gotchas, Claude keeps rediscovering them the hard way. See _Where Things Go Wrong_.

**Quick wins to try:** You're already building custom skills, so create a single `/status` skill that consolidates your frequent status checks and inventory views into one command — those are your most common session openers. Also try setting up hooks to auto-run verification steps after edits and deploys, which would catch the wrong-target and broken-filename issues that currently require a second pass. See _Features to Try_.

**Ambitious workflows:** As models get more capable, your multi-attempt deployment cycles (Vercel auth fixes, image fidelity checks, encoding retries) should collapse into single-command self-healing pipelines that detect failures, fix them, and retry autonomously. Your 36-command installation session and multi-hour sprint planning could also run as parallel agent swarms — simultaneous discovery, installation, and architectural auditing — so you shift from debugger-in-the-loop to reviewer-of-finished-work. See _On the Horizon_.

